  "final": "The duplicated pipeline now lives in one shared module, `src/exporter.py`, and both public wrappers delegate to it. The existing check passes, and the output matches the original implementation on every case I tried.\n\n**Changes**\n- **`src/exporter.py`** (new):\n  - `_normalize(rows)` keeps only `id` and `name`, in that order. Missing or `None` values become `''`, other values are passed through `str()`, and it builds a new list so inputs are never mutated and one-shot iterables work.\n  - `_serialize_csv` writes an `id,name` header with LF line endings and standard CSV quoting. It also handles the empty case.\n  - `_serialize_json` uses `ensure_ascii=False` and `separators=(',', ':')`.\n  - `export_rows(rows, format)` dispatches on `'csv'` or `'json'`. Any other value, including non-strings, raises `ValueError`.\n- **`src/csv_exporter.py`** and **`src/json_exporter.py`**: each is now a one-line call to `export_rows(rows, \"csv\")` or `export_rows(rows, \"json\")`. Their normalization and serialization loops are removed.\n- **`src/dispatcher.py`**: unchanged. It still calls the wrappers and still raises its own `ValueError` for unknown formats, so it keeps working.\n\n**Verification**\n- `python3 checks/public.py` passes.\n- Compared against the original `export_csv` and `export_json` implementations on empty input, `None`/missing values, non-string values, embedded quotes, commas, newlines, `\\r\\n`, and non-ASCII text. Output was identical.\n- Confirmed that a one-shot iterator gives the same result as a list and that the input rows are left unchanged.\n- Confirmed `export_rows` raises `ValueError` for `'xml'`, `None`, and `1`.\n\nI did not add new check files under `checks/`, since the existing public check already covers the main behavior and the equivalence checks above were one-off scripts. Say if you want them added as permanent checks.",
